# Safety guard tier roster - EXAMPLE
#
# Copy this to one of the paths the guard reads, in this order:
#   1. $SAFETY_GUARD_ROSTER          (whatever you point it at)
#   2. .claude/safety-roster         (this project, gitignored)
#   3. ~/.claude/safety-roster       (this machine, all projects)
#
#   cp .claude/safety-roster.example .claude/safety-roster
#
# The real file is gitignored on purpose. It carries who is trusted on this
# machine, which is a per-machine decision and not a repository fact.
#
# A key that is PRESENT here REPLACES the built-in list for that tier rather
# than extending it. That is what makes a promotion one line in one file. The
# guard script is meant to be deployed byte-identically to every location your
# runtime reads, so a promotion must never be an edit in N copies of a script.
#
# Identities are version-control committer emails, lowercased, compared exactly.
# Check yours with: git config user.email
#
# An identity that is in neither list is on the team tier. An identity that does
# not resolve at all is unresolved, which behaves the same as team on every cell
# of the matrix: strictest, never friendliest. That is deliberate, because a
# scheduled or unattended run is exactly the context where identity is missing.
#
# The tiers:
#   owner    Built the system and carries the consequences. Gets a warning
#            instead of a block on destructive internal actions, and a block on
#            anything irreversible.
#   trusted  Runs live work daily and is accountable for it. Confirms on
#            everything guarded.
#   (team)   Everyone else, including every unrecognised identity. Confirms on
#            destructive internal work, needs two-person approval on anything
#            irreversible. Not listed here, it is the default.

owner: you@yourcompany.com
trusted: revops-lead@yourcompany.com, ae-manager@yourcompany.com

# Hostnames of the live systems you can reach from a shell. Any shell command
# mentioning one of these is treated as an irreversible outward action, which
# closes the hole where a blocked action is retried as a direct API call.
# Leave this empty until you have real hostnames: a wrong entry here blocks a
# working command, and a deny with false positives gets the gate switched off.
guarded-hosts: api.yourcrm.example, api.youroutboundtool.example
